1. The Hammer: fracture words into operators

Natural-language adjectives are overlapping, contextual descriptors, not mutually exclusive brain species. Each label is a hypothesis about behavior and/or its evaluation.

|Label              |Primary operator                                                  |Necessary caution                                     |
|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
|Intelligent        |Learn, reason, transfer, generalize, adapt                        |Broad construct; no single behavior proves it         |
|Smart              |Succeed cognitively relative to task or everyday standard         |Informal umbrella term; specify criterion             |
|Knowledgeable      |Store, organize, retrieve relevant knowledge                      |Information can be stale, shallow, or misapplied      |
|Educated           |Acquire structured instruction                                    |Credentials do not guarantee understanding            |
|Experienced        |Acquire practice-based priors and tacit patterns                  |Experience can entrench bad habits                    |
|Clever             |Reframe constraints to find a surprising effective route          |Novelty may sacrifice robustness or ethics            |
|Creative           |Generate valuable novelty                                         |Novelty and usefulness must be judged separately      |
|Insightful         |Expose hidden structure or significant relationships              |A seductive explanation may be false                  |
|Perceptive         |Extract consequential signal from noisy observations              |Sensitivity can amplify irrelevant cues               |
|Intuitive          |Rapidly match or synthesize patterns without explicit deliberation|Transfer outside learned domain can fail              |
|Analytical         |Decompose and explicitly evaluate hypotheses                      |Slow analysis can miss timing or tacit knowledge      |
|Rational           |Update and choose coherently given evidence and goals             |Reasoning can be internally coherent on false premises|
|Wise               |Evaluate ends, means, uncertainty, timing, and consequences       |Value-laden; do not reduce to IQ or certainty         |
|Prudent            |Preserve options and proportion risk to stakes                    |Excessive caution can also be costly                  |
|Strategic          |Sequence choices across horizons, actors, and dependencies        |Can miss local reality                                |
|Tactical           |Select effective near-term actions under conditions               |Local success may harm long-term goals                |
|Cunning            |Use asymmetries of expectations, information, or incentives       |Describes method, not moral worth                     |
|Shrewd             |Detect practical constraints, leverage, and incentives            |Accuracy requires context and evidence                |
|Resourceful        |Repurpose means under scarcity or surprise                        |Improvisation needs evaluation and limits             |
|Astute             |Detect consequential distinctions and judge accurately            |Domain-specific, not universal                        |
|Sharp              |Process or respond quickly and precisely                          |Speed is not always accuracy                          |
|Brilliant / Genius |Exceptional performance or accomplishment by a standard           |Evaluative labels, not atomic faculties               |
|Emotionally skilled|Recognize, communicate about, and regulate emotions               |Not reducible to agreeableness or manipulation        |
|Metacognitive      |Monitor thinking, uncertainty, errors, and mode selection         |Overmonitoring can impede performance                 |

Semantic parsing rule: When encountering a label, ask: What observable transformation makes this description apt in this context? What would falsify it? What else could explain the behavior?

Do not infer global trait ranks from one feat; do not infer ability, trustworthiness, or moral character from a person’s identity, diagnosis, profession, disability, ethnicity, or neurological label.

1.1 From nouns to transformations

Translate descriptors into functional verbs:

• Perceptive: noise → relevant signal.
• Knowledgeable: cue → accessible knowledge.
• Intelligent: unfamiliar evidence → useful model or adaptable solution.
• Clever: constraint → alternative possibility.
• Creative: existing elements → valuable new relation or artifact.
• Insightful: disparate observations → explanatory structure.
• Cunning: expectation/information asymmetry → advantageous move; apply ethical review.
• Strategic: objectives + horizon + dependencies → sequenced plan.
• Tactical: immediate situation + resources → situated action.
• Wise: possible ends + consequences + values → defensible priorities and restraint.
• Metacognitive: cognition + feedback → calibrated mode switch or revision.

Useful distinction: Intelligence is one ingredient of cognitive performance; wisdom participates in choosing and revising what performance should serve.

