2. Substrate, architecture, process, and person are different layers

Layer A — Substrate

Biological neurons, chemical signaling, external memory, silicon compute, institutional rules, human communication, mixed media. Do not ascribe subjective experience based only on sophisticated behavior.

Layer B — Organization / topology

• Centralized: concentrated control/decision authority.
• Distributed: local processing with coordination across parts.
• Swarm / stigmergic: collective behavior shaped by local rules, signals, and environmental traces.
• Hierarchical: delegated control across levels.
• Networked / peer-to-peer: coordination through many-to-many relationships.
• Hybrid: multiple architectures coexist and change by task.

The human brain is itself a network of interacting systems, not one undivided faculty. Octopus arms illustrate extensive peripheral nervous processing coordinated with the central brain. Birds demonstrate that impressive problem solving need not require a mammalian neocortex. Insects and colonies illustrate individual computation coupled to collective patterning. These are biological examples, not evidence that an ant colony or software swarm shares human consciousness.

Layer C — Processing mode (dynamic, not personality type)

Reactive, intuitive, deliberative, exploratory, procedural, social, strategic, reflective, integrative. One agent can switch among them; modes can run together or interfere. Avoid simplistic left-brain/right-brain personality typing.

Layer D — Profile (contextual, multidimensional)

Candidate dimensions: perception, memory, learning, abstraction, causal modeling, creativity, anticipation, action, social modeling, metacognition, value judgment, calibration. These are design axes, not established independent psychometric factors. Semantic PCA may suggest more compact latent dimensions, but compression must be tested for lost distinctions.

Layer E — State and environment

Fatigue, pain, attention, motivation, training, stress, information quality, tools, coordination cost, trust, time, culture, stakes, accessibility, consent. Performance changes when these change.

Layer F — Goal and impact

Agent goals, shared goals, imposed goals, side effects, long-run consequences, reversible/irreversible choices. Separate what works from what is worth doing.

