6. Transfer atlas: from brains to everywhere else

Transfer relational operations, not superficial labels. Each transfer requires source pattern → target mechanism → preserved invariant → broken assumptions → distinct test.

A. Distributed nervous systems → edge organizations

• Source: Local sensory-motor processing coordinated with wider behavioral priorities (e.g., octopus arms).
• Target: Small autonomous teams that can respond locally without constant headquarters approval.
• Invariant: Locally useful processing plus coordination boundaries.
• Do not import: Claims that teams physically resemble nerve tissue or have identical motivation.
• Test: Compare decision latency and error under centralized vs scoped-local authority.

B. Corvid problem solving → substrate independence

• Source: Capabilities achieved by neural organization unlike mammalian neocortex.
• Target: AI or organizational designs using different mechanisms for a similar function.
• Invariant: Evaluate competence at the function, not familiarity of hardware.
• Do not import: Assumptions of equal general intelligence or subjective experience.
• Test: Compare generalization and robustness across changed tasks, not one showcase.

C. Ant colonies → distributed logistics

• Source: Local signaling, environmental traces, feedback, collective patterning.
• Target: Routing work, queues, resource allocation, or swarm algorithms.
• Invariant: Simple local feedback can produce useful system-level coordination.
• Do not import: Proof of sentience, benevolence, or automatic global optimality.
• Test: Compare throughput, congestion, resilience, and failure recovery.

D. Cleverness versus wisdom → business growth

• Source: Finding an efficient route versus scrutinizing the destination and externalities.
• Target: Wild Hawg launch strategy or any growth-oriented business.
• Invariant: Local optimization and long-term legitimacy are different objectives.
• Test: Compare acquisition gains alongside customer trust, complaints, repeat purchases, and sustainability; reject misleading growth tactics.

E. Cognitive profiles → self-conducting ensembles

• Source: Complementary perception, knowledge, invention, foresight, judgment, and execution.
• Target: Human crews or multi-agent AI workflows.
• Invariant: Role-task fit and translation may matter more than appointing a universally ‘smartest’ leader.
• Test: Compare solo, parallel, and coordinated configurations with consistent tasks, cost, and quality criteria; allow voluntary handoffs and exit.

F. Metacognition → adaptive orchestration

• Source: Detecting limits of a current strategy and changing modes.
• Target: Model routing, escalation, tool selection, or personal learning plans.
• Invariant: A system may improve by detecting when its current operator is inappropriate.
• Test: Deliberately introduce tasks where the default specialist underperforms; record whether a mode switch improves accuracy net of overhead.

G. Tacit expertise → apprenticeship and skill transfer

• Source: Expert action can depend on practiced patterns difficult to verbalize.
• Target: Teaching crafts, navigation, parenting skills, sports, or AI-assisted instruction.
• Invariant: Verbal explanation is not identical to embodied competence.
• Test: Compare explanations, demonstrations, supervised practice, and performance on novel variants.

H. Living Atlas → problem-dependent cognitive maps

• Source: Different representations emphasize different relationships.
• Target: A task-specific ‘map of minds’ that changes with the problem.
• Invariant: Relevance, connection costs, and centrality depend on the chosen lens.
• Test: See whether the same roster needs different coupling for emergency response, innovation, teaching, and long-run governance.

I. Cleverness + cunning → incentive design / security defense

• Source: Attentiveness to information asymmetries and strategic expectations.
• Target: Authorized red-teaming, fraud prevention, negotiation analysis, resilient system design.
• Invariant: Predictable expectations can create exploitable failure points.
• Boundary: Use for consent-based testing and defense, not covert manipulation of real people.
• Test: Test resilience with approved simulations and documented constraints.

J. Family adventures → scaffolded capability development

• Source: Expertise grows through progressively challenging situations with feedback.
• Target: Age-appropriate outdoor skills, games, stories, navigation, craft, and teamwork.
• Invariant: Capability develops through supported participation and increasing agency.
• Do not import: Permanent labels or ranking children by ‘brain type’.
• Test: Track enjoyable, voluntary skill progress and confidence, not speculative fixed intelligence scores.

