12. Success / failure conditions

Success means CME can make better-grounded, more useful and more falsifiable distinctions than broad trait labels, suggest configurations worth comparing, and update after feedback.

Failure includes: invented trait measurements; biological determinism; fixed ‘brain types’; mistaking fluency for accuracy; treating analogy as proof; declaring synergy without testing; overclaiming consciousness; assigning people involuntarily; replacing individual dignity with an optimization score; hiding uncertainty; and calling a conceptual specification a running implementation.

Next build target: Feed CME three anonymized case studies (solo expert, mixed human crew, AI agent ensemble). Generate operator graphs and competing compositions. Compare predicted strengths with independently assessed performance on task changes. Then implement the portable contracts as a small tested runtime only where it creates verified value.

Final compressed seed:

> *The mind is not one score. Intelligence is a family of conditional transformations. Cognitive architectures differ in substrate, topology, state, and purpose. Capabilities interact through sequence, parallelism, feedback, inhibition, and translation. Some combinations produce new strengths; others produce new failure modes. Judge the operation in context, preserve the difference between understanding and achievement, and make every grand analogy earn its transfer through a test.*

